{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Sqrt","lean:module:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityCountingBound"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicity_not_extensive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicity_not_extensive","kind":"theorem","line":273,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L273-L284","doc":"**The exclusion, assembled.** Under a Hardy–Ramanujan-type bound on the\nnumber of summands, the multiplicity surplus is strictly less than the extensive\nterm `a·A` for every `A` past the exhibited threshold `(c/a)²`. So the\nmultiplicity channel of a trace-sum binding law cannot be the extensive term —\non any graph, at any scale, with any signs, for any braid word. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.hardyRamanujan_gives_sqrt_law","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.hardyRamanujan_gives_sqrt_law","kind":"theorem","line":262,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L262-L271","doc":"**Hardy–Ramanujan gives the `√` law.** If the number of summands `m` of the\n`A`-nucleon trace sum obeys `log m ≤ c·√A` — which the partition count `p(3A)`\ndoes, with `c = π√2`, by Hardy–Ramanujan — then the multiplicity surplus obeys\nthe same bound, and hence (`sqrt_lt_linear_of_sq_lt`) is strictly below any\npositive linear rate beyond an explicit threshold. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_per_nucleon_antitone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_per_nucleon_antitone","kind":"theorem","line":239,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L239-L254","doc":"**Per nucleon, a `√` law decays.** `c√A / A = c/√A` is non-increasing in\n`A`. The measured `B/A` is flat across the mass table — that is what nuclear\n*saturation* means — so the two have different shapes, again with no scale in\nthe statement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_law_undershoots","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_law_undershoots","kind":"theorem","line":227,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L227-L237","doc":"The shortfall is a genuine one — strict — as soon as the mass ratio exceeds\none. This is the falsifiable edge of `sqrt_law_shortfall`: matching at `A₁`\nforces an undershoot at every larger `A₂`, for every positive scale. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_law_shortfall","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_law_shortfall","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L216-L225","doc":"**The shortfall of a `√` law against saturation is exactly `√(A₂/A₁)`,\nindependent of the scale and of the constant.** Normalise any `c√A` law to\nreproduce the binding `B₁` at mass number `A₁`; it then predicts\n`B₁·√(A₂/A₁)` at `A₂`, while a saturating (constant `B/A`) law gives\n`B₁·(A₂/A₁)`. The identity below says the second is `√(A₂/A₁)` times the first,\nexactly — an equality, so no tolerance and no fit is involved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.card_ge_exp_of_linear_le_log_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.card_ge_exp_of_linear_le_log_card","kind":"theorem","line":198,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L198-L207","doc":"**The contrapositive with teeth.** If the multiplicity channel is to supply\na term linear in `A` at rate `a`, the number of summands must be at least\n`exp (a·A)` — exponential in the nucleon count. The partitions of `3A` number\n`exp(O(√A))`, so this is not a bound that a larger search could meet; it is a\nbound on the index set itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_lt_linear_of_sq_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.sqrt_lt_linear_of_sq_lt","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L178-L196","doc":"**A `c√A` law falls strictly below any positive linear rate once\n`A > (c/a)²`.** No limits: the threshold is exhibited. With `c = π√2` from\nHardy–Ramanujan and `a` the required per-nucleon rate in units of `E₀`, this is\nwhat makes the exclusion quantitative at every physical mass number rather than\nmerely asymptotic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicitySurplus_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicitySurplus_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L167-L174","doc":"The surplus is non-negative when the sum is at least its dominant summand —\nthe other side of the sandwich, recorded so the surplus is visibly a *channel*\nwith a floor as well as a cap and not merely an error term. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicitySurplus_le_log_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicitySurplus_le_log_card","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L157-L165","doc":"**The multiplicity surplus is at most `log (card)`.** With `T ≤ m · M` and\nboth `T` and `M` positive, the log of the ratio is at most `log m`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicitySurplus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.MultiplicityNotExtensive.multiplicitySurplus","kind":"def","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/MultiplicityNotExtensive.lean#L149-L155","doc":"The **multiplicity surplus**: how much the sum's log-amplitude exceeds the\nlog-amplitude of its own largest summand. Under the archimedean bridge\n(`ArchimedeanTraceBridge`) binding is `E₀` times a log-amplitude, so this is the\npart of the binding that multiplicity — as opposed to any single partition's\nweight — could be responsible for. ","signatureChunk":0}]}